Yes — *Streptococcus sanguinis* can leave the mouth and reach distant sites, but the pathways and risks differ by organ.

Normal role in the mouth
S. sanguinis (formerly S. sanguis) is a common, generally beneficial oral commensal. It is an early colonizer of dental plaque, helps shape a healthier biofilm, and competes with more harmful species such as S. mutans (cavity-causing bacteria). It lives on teeth, gums, and in saliva.

How it “sheds” / leaves the mouth
It can enter the bloodstream (transient bacteremia) through:
Everyday activities (chewing, brushing, flossing)
Gum inflammation or periodontal disease (inflamed tissues make entry easier)
Dental procedures (cleanings, extractions, etc.)

Once in the blood, it can travel systemically. It is also swallowed in saliva (reaching the stomach/gut) or can be aspirated into the airways.

Effects on specific organs

Heart (strongest and best-documented link)  
S. sanguinis is one of the classic causes of infective endocarditis (infection of the heart valves or endocardium), especially subacute bacterial endocarditis. It ranks among the most common viridans-group streptococci involved (often 18–30% of oral streptococcal endocarditis cases).  

It can adhere to platelets and damaged valve tissue, form vegetations, and trigger inflammation. People with pre-existing valve problems, congenital heart issues, or prosthetic valves are at higher risk. Animal studies also show it can promote aortic inflammation and accelerate atherosclerosis.

Lungs  
Oral streptococci (including S. sanguinis / viridans group) can reach the lungs mainly by aspiration of saliva or plaque, especially in people with swallowing difficulties, reduced consciousness, or during sleep. They contribute to aspiration pneumonia, lung abscesses, or empyema in susceptible individuals. Direct hematogenous spread is less common than for endocarditis.

Stomach / Gut  
Large amounts of oral bacteria (including streptococci) are swallowed daily in saliva. Most are killed by stomach acid, but some can survive in biofilms or food particles and reach the intestines. This can contribute to microbial load, mild inflammation, or altered gut fermentation in some people. Specific, strong evidence linking S. sanguinis alone to major stomach or gut disease is weaker than for the heart; other oral pathogens (e.g., Porphyromonas gingivalis, Fusobacterium) have clearer gut associations. Rare cases of S. sanguinis bacteremia linked to GI lesions exist.

Brain  
Direct infection (e.g., meningitis or brain abscess) is uncommon for S. sanguinis specifically (more typical of the S. anginosus group). However:
Bacteremia from endocarditis can send septic emboli to the brain, causing stroke, mycotic aneurysms, or abscesses.
Oral streptococcal DNA (viridans group) has been found in cerebral thrombi of some stroke patients.
Chronic low-grade bacteremia and inflammation from oral sources are studied as possible contributors to neuroinflammation, though the evidence is still developing and not specific to S. sanguinis alone.
Reducing oral bacterial load and biofilm (via thorough hygiene) lowers the chance of these bacteria entering the bloodstream or being swallowed/aspirated in large numbers. The strongest clinical link remains to the heart (endocarditis), followed by aspiration-related lung issues.
